  "final_answer": "I found the information I need. Let me also verify the description text is complete by reading more carefully.\n\n**Summary of findings:**\n\nThe most recent open-source NLP model for medical summarization on Hugging Face is:\n\n**Model**: `Chilliwiddit/Openi-llama3.1-8B-WeightedLoss-medical5`\n\n**Description**: \"Basically used to summarize text from the Open-i dataset\"\n\n**Key Details**:\n- **Pipeline Tag**: Summarization\n- **Base Model**: meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B\n- **Library Tags**: PEFT, Safetensors, Transformers, LoRA (English language)\n- **Updated**: February 3, 2026\n\n**Training Details**:\n- **Training Data**: The Open-i dataset (an open-source collection of medical/radiology images and reports)\n- **Training Hyperparameters**:\n  - 16-bit Mixed Precision\n  - Learning Rate (LR) of 0.0–1\n  - 5 Epochs\n  - Lambda medical weight: 9\n  - Lambda negation weight: 0.3\n  - Used the 2nd iteration of a \"summary medical concepts\" file\n\n**Architecture**: It is a PEFT/LoRA adapter (fine-tuned on top of Llama-3.1-8B), with 61 referenced files, also available in a quantized variant (`unsloth/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-bnb-4bit`).\n\nIn essence, this is a LoRA fine-tune of Meta's Llama-3.1-8B Instruct model, specifically adapted using a weighted-loss scheme (heavier weight on medical content) to summarize medical text from the Open-i dataset — a well-known radiology/medical imaging report corpus.\n\n---\n\n## Final Answer\n\nThe most recent open-source NLP model released on Hugging Face for medical summarization is **`Chilliwiddit/Openi-llama3.1-8B-WeightedLoss-medical5`** (updated February 3, 2026).\n\n**Description**: *\"Basically used to summarize text from the Open-i dataset.\"*\n\nIt is a **PEFT/LoRA fine-tune of Meta's Llama-3.1-8B** model, trained on the **Open-i dataset** (an open-source medical/radiology report dataset) for 5 epochs using 16-bit mixed precision, a learning rate of 0.0–1, a lambda medical weight of 9, and a lambda negation weight of 0.3. The model's purpose is to summarize medical text, with the weighted loss emphasizing medical content over negation terms during training.",
